[# Section 16 — capabilities: the deny-by-default permission model and
   the --allow-* grant flags. Spec home: spec/03-approved/core/security.md
   (model), spec/03-approved/misc/cli.md §3.7 (CLI flags). Every command
   and output on this page was run against the live binary (issue #425
   coverage gap #1). #]

[section id=capabilities n=16 title='Capabilities and permissions'

  [intro
    """A CX evaluation runs under an explicit **capability set**, and the
       set is empty unless you grant something: **deny-by-default, no
       ambient authority**. Pure computation — parsing, canonical emit,
       CXPath selection, in-memory transforms — needs no capability at
       all. Any operation with an external effect (reading a file,
       dialing a host, spawning a process, even reading the clock)
       checks the active set at the effect point and raises the
       capability error `CXER0271` when the matching grant is absent.
       The model is specified in `spec/core/security.md`; this section
       is the practical guide to the `--allow-*` flags."""]

  [child id=caps-model n=16.1 title='Deny-by-default'
    [body
      """Run a program with no flags and it can compute anything but
         touch nothing. This program tries to read a file:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/io']
[$io:read-file 'VERSION']"""]
    [body
      """Without a grant, the read is refused at the effect point —
         before any filesystem access happens:"""]
    [output
      """[err code=cx-err:CXER0271 message='E_CAP_DENIED: read capability required for io-read-file; none granted (grant via --allow-read)']"""]
    [body
      """The error is **actionable by contract**: it names the missing
         capability, the operation that wanted it, and the exact flag
         that grants it. Add the grant and the same program works:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx read_version.cx --allow-read
'0.12.0
'"""]
    [body
      """Grants combine — a program that reads the environment and
         writes a file needs both flags:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx snapshot_home.cx --allow-env --allow-write"""]
    [note
      """A denied effect is refused **fail-closed, before any type or
         domain validation** — the path is never touched, the socket
         never opened. And because the check happens at the effect
         point, a `pure` function can never raise `CXER0271` under any
         capability set: purity is capability-freedom by construction
         (the effect-totality rule in `spec/core/security.md`)."""]]

  [child id=caps-grants n=16.2 title='The grant list'
    [body
      """Nine capabilities cover every effectful surface in the engine
         and standard library, plus one blanket opt-out. This is the
         full set the binary accepts — there are no hidden grants, and
         a misspelled grant flag (`--alow-net`, `--allow-nett`, …) is a
         hard usage error (exit 2) naming the flag, never a silent
         no-grant:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'flag'] [th 'capability'] [th 'gates']]
      [tr [td '--allow-read'] [td 'read'] [td 'filesystem reads, [?cx include], terminal input']]
      [tr [td '--allow-write'] [td 'write'] [td 'filesystem writes']]
      [tr [td '--allow-net[=host[:port]]'] [td 'net'] [td 'HTTP client and server, sockets, network stores; see the scoping subsection']]
      [tr [td '--allow-env'] [td 'env'] [td 'environment-variable reads']]
      [tr [td '--allow-clock'] [td 'clock'] [td 'wall-clock reads, profiling timers, non-mock [?sleep]']]
      [tr [td '--allow-random'] [td 'random'] [td 'CSPRNG / OS entropy — crypto, uuid, random']]
      [tr [td '--allow-subprocess'] [td 'subprocess'] [td 'process spawn ([$process:run] and friends)']]
      [tr [td '--allow-eval'] [td 'eval'] [td 'dynamic evaluation — [?eval] tree-eval']]
      [tr [td '--allow-secret-reveal'] [td 'secret-reveal'] [td 'declassifying a [?secret] value via [?reveal]']]
      [tr [td '--allow-all'] [td '(all)'] [td 'every capability — explicit trusted-local opt-out']]]
    [body
      """Each denial names its own grant. A few worked examples, each
         run live. Reading the environment:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/env']
[$env:var 'HOME']"""]
    [output
      """$ cx home.cx
[err code=cx-err:CXER0271 message='E_CAP_DENIED: env capability required for env-var; none granted (grant via --allow-env)']
$ cx home.cx --allow-env
'/Users/ep'"""]
    [body """Reading the clock:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/time']
[$time:now]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx now.cx --allow-clock
'2026-07-14T22:11:55.071838Z'"""]
    [body """Asking for entropy:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/uuid']
[$uuid:v4]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx id.cx --allow-random
'2c9be10f-2a88-456f-b00d-9af606793775'"""]
    [body """Spawning a subprocess:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/process']
[$process:run ('echo', 'hi')]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx run_echo.cx --allow-subprocess
[proc-result exit-code=0 signaled=false timed-out=false stdout='hi
' stderr='']"""]
    [body
      """Evaluating a constructed tree — dynamic evaluation is a
         capability like any other, so a program that builds and runs
         code at runtime says so on the command line:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?eval [?quote [+ $a $b]] [context {a: 10, b: 5}]]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx calc.cx --allow-eval
15"""]
    [body
      """Secrets are the inverse case: **wrapping** a value with
         [?secret] needs no grant, and the wrapped value renders
         redacted everywhere — logs, output, serialization:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?let [= $token [?secret 'hunter2']]
  $token]"""]
    [output
      """'‹redacted›'"""]
    [body
      """Only **declassifying** it is gated: `[?reveal $token]` raises
         `CXER0271` unless the program runs with
         `--allow-secret-reveal`."""]
    [note
      """Grant scoping syntax such as `--allow-read=./data` or
         `--allow-env=HOME` is accepted on the command line, but in the
         current engine only the **net** grant enforces its scope
         (next subsection). For every other capability the resource
         suffix is carried, not yet enforced — the grant is
         all-or-nothing per capability. Per-path and per-name
         enforcement is specified (`spec/core/security.md`, coarse-v1
         scoping) and lands per domain; do not rely on a path suffix as
         a security boundary today."""]]

  [child id=caps-net n=16.3 title='Scoping the network grant'
    [body
      """`--allow-net` is the one grant with enforced resource scoping,
         because it is the one most worth narrowing. Three levels:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'flag'] [th 'meaning']]
      [tr [td '(none)'] [td 'every dial and bind raises CXER0271 naming the host:port it wanted']]
      [tr [td '--allow-net'] [td 'any public host — but private, loopback, and link-local ranges stay denied (CXER4504)']]
      [tr [td '--allow-net=HOST[:PORT]'] [td 'least privilege: only the named host (and port) is dialable; a literal-IP or localhost scope also admits that private address']]]
    [body
      """The private-range deny set on the bare grant is deliberate:
         `--allow-net` means "talk to the internet", not "probe my
         loopback and LAN". A program handed a URL that resolves into
         `127.0.0.0/8`, `10.0.0.0/8`, `192.168.0.0/16`, and the other
         reserved ranges gets a refusal:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/http']
[$http:get 'http://127.0.0.1:1/health']"""]
    [output
      """$ cx probe.cx --allow-net
[err code=cx-err:CXER4504 message='E_NET_FORBIDDEN_ADDRESS: 127.0.0.1:1 resolves into a denied range with no admitting literal-IP/localhost grant (§4.5)']"""]
    [body
      """To talk to a local service, name it — a literal-IP or
         `localhost` scope overrides the deny set for exactly that
         address:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx probe.cx --allow-net=127.0.0.1:8443"""]
    [body
      """And a host-scoped grant is a real boundary: with
         `--allow-net=api.example.com:443`, a dial to any other host is
         refused with a `CXER0271` naming the host and port it
         wanted — so the denial tells you exactly what to review before
         widening the grant."""]]

  [child id=caps-allow-all n=16.4 title='The --allow-all opt-out'
    [body
      """`--allow-all` grants everything. It exists because
         deny-by-default must stay ergonomic for fully-trusted local
         use — your own build script, a REPL experiment, a one-off
         transform on your own machine. It is an explicit, visible
         opt-out: anyone reading the invocation can see the program
         runs unconfined."""]
    [body
      """Where **not** to use it: anything long-running, anything
         network-facing, anything whose inputs you do not control, and
         any invocation you copy into documentation, CI, a systemd
         unit, or a Dockerfile. Those should carry the narrowest set
         that works — the flags are the audit trail. Note that
         `--allow-all` is strictly wider than granting all nine flags
         individually: it is also the only grant that bypasses the
         private-range deny set on network dials."""]]

  [child id=caps-narrowing n=16.5 title='Narrowing with [?with-caps]'
    [body
      """A program can **narrow** its own set for a dynamic extent —
         never widen it. `[?with-caps]` takes one or more
         `[deny CAP]` clauses and a body; inside the body the denied
         capabilities are gone, even if the process was launched with
         them, and they come back when the body exits:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/env']
[?with-caps [deny env]
  [$env:var 'HOME']]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx narrowed.cx --allow-env
[err code=cx-err:CXER0271 message='E_CAP_DENIED: env capability required for env-var; none granted (grant via --allow-env)']"""]
    [body
      """This is the tool for running untrusted sub-computations:
         evaluate a tree you did not author under
         `[?with-caps [deny net] [deny subprocess] …]` and its
         authority is bounded no matter what it contains. A `deny`
         cannot be undone inside the body — narrowing is
         one-way by design."""]]

  [child id=caps-errors n=16.6 title='Denials are values'
    [body
      """`CXER0271` is a normal error **value**, not a crash. It flows
         through the program like any other value, so a program can
         detect a missing grant and degrade gracefully with
         `[?match]` (or `[?else]` / `[?fallback]`):"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/io']
[?match [$io:read-file 'config.cx']
  [case [err $e] 'no read grant or no config - using defaults']
  [else 'loaded config']]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx degrade.cx
'no read grant or no config - using defaults'"""]
    [body
      """What a denial can never do is succeed silently: there is no
         fallback value, no partial effect, no environment where the
         denied operation "sort of" ran. Either the grant is present
         and the effect happens, or you hold an `[err]` naming the
         grant."""]
    [note
      """This guide eats the same dog food: every `cx` example on these
         pages is executed against the live binary by the docs-example
         gate, which grants **no** capabilities — a snippet whose only
         output is a `CXER0271` denial passes (the denial is
         environmental, not staleness), and anything else that errors
         fails the build."""]]
]
